 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Diffusion-regularized NeRF](7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md)
-Mitigates severe under-constraint in sparse-view training by using a Denoising Diffusion Model (DDM) to regularize geometry and color. It sets itself apart from [RegNeRF](8582-regnerf.md) by using a DDM trained on RGBD patches rather than normalizing flows on RGB patches, and from [Depth and Geometry Supervision](8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md) by learning a probabilistic prior over RGBD distributions instead of relying on explicit depth sensors.
+Mitigates severe under-constraint in sparse-view training by using a Denoising Diffusion Model (DDM) trained on RGBD patches to regularize geometry and color, distinguishing itself from [RegNeRF](8582-regnerf.md) which uses normalizing flows on RGB patches. Choose this when dealing with limited input views where "floaters" are prevalent and a suitable RGBD diffusion prior is available.
 
 ### [RegNeRF](8582-regnerf.md)
-Synthesizes novel views from sparse images by applying depth and color regularization constraints without scene-specific pretraining. It distinguishes itself from siblings relying on pretrained feature extractors or adversarial training by using a Normalizing Flow model and a smoothness prior to constrain unobserved views, achieving performance comparable to pretrained baselines.
+Applies depth and color regularization constraints using a Normalizing Flow model and smoothness prior to stabilize NeRF optimization with sparse inputs, avoiding the need for scene-specific pretraining. Choose this when a large external dataset (e.g., JFT-300M) is available to pretrain the flow model, providing a generative prior for unobserved views.
 
 ### [Depth and Geometry Supervision](8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md)
-Accelerates optimization and improves geometric accuracy in sparse-view settings by incorporating external depth estimates or internal geometric consistency constraints. Unlike pure photometric methods that suffer from shape-radiance ambiguity, these techniques constrain the density field $\sigma$ and rendering weights $w_i$ to align with known surface locations, reducing overfitting and preventing degenerate solutions such as "floaters" or image copying.
+Accelerates optimization and improves geometric accuracy by incorporating external depth estimates or internal geometric priors into the training loss, reducing shape-radiance ambiguity. Choose this when explicit depth sensors or stereo matches are available, or when geometric consistency is prioritized over pure photometric fidelity.
 
 ### [Specific Loss Formulations](10636-specific-loss-formulations.md)
-Encompasses specific mathematical formulations for regularization and reconstruction terms added to the total loss. It addresses issues such as high-frequency detail loss due to misalignment, distributed computation of distortion metrics, and frequency inconsistency in few-shot settings. Unlike siblings relying on external geometric baselines or diffusion priors, these methods define explicit loss structures or distributed aggregation protocols.
+Encompasses explicit mathematical formulations for regularization and reconstruction terms, addressing issues like high-frequency detail loss and distributed computation of distortion metrics. Choose this when standard losses fail to capture specific artifacts or when distributed training requires custom aggregation protocols.
 
 ### [Multiplex Training via S3IM](10894-multiplex-training-via-s3im.md)
-Augments standard photometric losses with a Stochastic Structural SIMilarity (S3IM) term to improve reconstruction quality for complex details and sparse inputs. It distinguishes itself from standard SSIM optimization by capturing non-local structural information from distant pixels across different images via stochastic patching, whereas standard SSIM only captures local structural information from nearby pixels within a single image.
+Augments standard photometric losses with a Stochastic Structural SIMilarity (S3IM) term that captures non-local structural information from distant pixels via stochastic patching, unlike standard SSIM which is local. Choose this when complex details and sparse inputs require global structural consistency enforcement.
 
 ### [S3IM (Stochastic Structural SIMilarity)](10895-s3im-stochastic-structural-similarity.md)
-A regularization term that captures nonlocal structural similarity by processing multiple data points (pixels/rays) as a collective set rather than individually. It addresses the limitation of point-wise losses like MSE, which fail to utilize the collective supervision of distant pixels. Unlike conventional pixel-wise losses, S3IM cannot be expressed as the sum of any loss computed over individual pixels from the batch, allowing it to enforce global structural consistency at nearly no additional computational cost.
+A regularization term that captures nonlocal structural similarity by processing multiple data points as a collective set, enforcing global structural consistency at nearly no additional computational cost compared to point-wise losses. Choose this when the batch of sampled rays lacks spatial coherence, allowing the method to treat random pixels as "stochastic patches" for SSIM computation.
+
+### [Volumetric Supervision](11100-volumetric-supervision.md)
+Leverages high-quality volumetric reconstructions from conventional tomography at boundary time steps to supervise neural network training at intermediate sparse-view steps, explicitly minimizing discrepancy with physically reconstructed targets. Choose this for dynamic scene reconstruction in sparse-view regimes where dense ground-truth volumes are available at start and end points.
 
 ### [Semantic Consistency Loss (CLIP-based)](11955-semantic-consistency-loss-clip-based.md)
-Aligns the semantic content of rendered images with ground-truth training images using features from the CLIP vision encoder. It solves photometric ambiguity in sparse-view settings by enforcing that the rendered output preserves high-level semantic structure. It differs from [Diffusion-regularized NeRF](7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md) by using a frozen, pre-trained 2D vision-language model (CLIP) rather than a generative diffusion prior, and from [RegNeRF](8582-regnerf.md) by operating in the semantic feature space rather than the geometric or photometric space.
+Aligns rendered images with ground-truth training images using features from a frozen CLIP vision encoder, enforcing high-level semantic structure preservation rather than just pixel-wise color values. Choose this when photometric ambiguity is high and semantic coherence is critical, such as in text-guided or sparse-view synthesis.
 
 ### Occlusion-Eliminating NeRF Variant
-- **Definition**: A NeRF variant composed of 2 layers: a scene MLP and a background MLP, to eliminate occlusions [S1].
-- **Steps**: Designs a cost volume loss strategy to guide the joint optimization of the scene MLP and camera parameters. Applies a depth bidirectional inconsistency prior in the background MLP to restore occlusion-free backgrounds via depth distance supervision [S1].
-- **Pitfall**: When the occluding object is a light-loss transparent glass body, reflection artifact noise pollution prevents generalization [S1].
