 ## Background
-Regularization in NeRF optimization emerged as a response to the inherent ill-posedness of the inverse rendering problem, particularly when training data is sparse. Early NeRF implementations relied heavily on photometric MSE loss, which often led to "floaters" (semi-transparent artifacts) and poor geometry in unobserved regions. The introduction of regularization techniques marked a shift towards incorporating prior knowledge—whether geometric, semantic, or distributional—into the optimization loop.
-
-Key developments include the use of Normalizing Flows for color regularization in sparse views (RegNeRF) [S1], the integration of diffusion models for RGBD consistency (Diffusion-regularized NeRF) [S1], and the use of semantic priors from vision-language models like CLIP [S1]. Other approaches have focused on geometric consistency through depth supervision [S1, 8687-depth-supervised-nerf.md] or structural similarity metrics like S3IM to capture non-local image structures [S1]. These methods collectively address the limitations of pure photometric optimization, enabling high-quality novel view synthesis from limited input data.
+The concept of regularization in NeRF optimization originated from the observation that standard photometric losses (MSE) are insufficient for sparse-view scenarios, leading to artifacts like "floaters" and degenerate geometry [S1, 8582-regnerf.md]. Early work by [RegNeRF](8582-regnerf.md) introduced normalizing flows to enforce geometric smoothness and color priors [S1]. Subsequent methods expanded this to include depth supervision [8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md], diffusion priors [7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md], and semantic consistency via CLIP [11955-semantic-consistency-loss-clip-based.md]. The field has evolved to include specialized losses for dynamic scenes [11100-volumetric-supervision.md], physically-based rendering constraints [S3], and confidence-based calibration [S7]. These methods are widely used in novel view synthesis, 3D generation, and inverse rendering pipelines where data is limited or noisy [S1, S2].
 
